Usage, use this when a task is complex.

529
00:25:00,300 --> 00:25:00,980
Example.

530
00:25:01,300 --> 00:25:03,180
Could you guide me on this report structure?

531
00:25:03,560 --> 00:25:05,160
I want to make sure I'm doing it correctly.

532
00:25:05,760 --> 00:25:08,890
This shows respect for your boss's experience.

533
00:25:08,910 --> 00:25:12,110
Next phrase, I've tried my best, but I'm still stuck.

534
00:25:12,810 --> 00:25:15,700
Meaning, this shows effort before asking for help.

535
00:25:16,520 --> 00:25:20,180
Usage, very important because bosses appreciate effort first.

536
00:25:20,940 --> 00:25:25,150
Example, I've tried my best, but I'm still stuck on the Excel formula.

537
00:25:25,430 --> 00:25:26,030
Can you help me?

538
00:25:26,670 --> 00:25:29,100
This builds trust and shows responsibility.

539
00:25:29,620 --> 00:25:32,400
Next phrase, can you point me in the right direction?

540
00:25:33,020 --> 00:25:36,640
Meaning, you are not asking for full answers, just guidance.

541
00:25:37,510 --> 00:25:40,550
Usage, great for developing independence.

542
00:25:40,570 --> 00:25:40,970
Example.

543
00:25:41,630 --> 00:25:44,060
Can you point me in the right direction for this presentation?

544
00:25:44,780 --> 00:25:47,420
This shows you want to learn, not depend fully.

545
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:51,160
Next phrase, I want to make sure I'm on the right track.

546
00:25:51,850 --> 00:25:54,810
Meaning, you are checking if your work is correct so far.

547
00:25:55,670 --> 00:25:59,000
Usage, use this during ongoing tasks.

548
00:25:59,020 --> 00:25:59,540
Example.

549
00:26:00,020 --> 00:26:02,970
I want to make sure I'm on the right track with the proposal draft.

550
00:26:03,610 --> 00:26:05,170
This prevents mistakes early.

551
00:26:05,190 --> 00:26:08,800
Now let's look at a full real-life office situation.

552
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:13,040
Imagine you are working on a client presentation and you are confused.

553
00:26:13,740 --> 00:26:15,470
Here's how the conversation might sound.

554
00:26:16,170 --> 00:26:17,810
Excuse me, do you have a moment?

555
00:26:18,510 --> 00:26:19,430
Yes, what's up?

556
00:26:20,070 --> 00:26:20,990
Sorry to bother you.

557
00:26:21,370 --> 00:26:26,020
I'm working on the client presentation, but I'm not sure how to structure the financial section.

558
00:26:26,820 --> 00:26:28,550
Okay, what part is confusing you?

559
00:26:29,020 --> 00:26:31,300
I've tried my best, but I'm still stuck.

560
00:26:31,860 --> 00:26:34,420
Could you guide me on what key points I should include?

561
00:26:35,200 --> 00:26:35,430
Sure.

562
00:26:35,910 --> 00:26:38,530
Focus on revenue growth and cost breakdown first.

563
00:26:39,190 --> 00:26:39,570
Thank you.

564
00:26:39,770 --> 00:26:40,530
That really helps.

565
00:26:41,130 --> 00:26:42,760
I want to make sure I'm on the right track.

566
00:26:43,500 --> 00:26:43,700
Good.

567
00:26:44,220 --> 00:26:45,480
Let me know if you need anything else.

568
00:26:46,160 --> 00:26:47,120
Thank you for your support.

569
00:26:47,970 --> 00:26:50,730
Now let's look at a full real-life office situation.

570
00:26:51,490 --> 00:26:54,300
Imagine you are working on conflicting priorities.

571
00:26:54,730 --> 00:26:55,950
Hi, do you have a moment?

572
00:26:56,630 --> 00:26:56,890
Yes.

573
00:26:57,780 --> 00:27:02,040
Sorry to bother you, but I have multiple deadlines this week, and I'm feeling a bit stuck.

574
00:27:02,740 --> 00:27:04,370
Which tasks are causing issues?

575
00:27:05,210 --> 00:27:08,390
I've tried my best, but I'm not sure how to prioritize them.

576
00:27:08,410 --> 00:27:10,530
Could you guide me on what I should focus on first?

577
00:27:11,320 --> 00:27:13,040
Focus on the client report first.

578
00:27:13,300 --> 00:27:14,200
That's the priority.

579
00:27:14,940 --> 00:27:15,240
Got it.

580
00:27:15,560 --> 00:27:16,260
That really helps.

581
00:27:16,640 --> 00:27:18,140
I want to make sure I'm on the right track.

582
00:27:18,870 --> 00:27:19,090
Good.

583
00:27:19,590 --> 00:27:21,230
Let me know if you need anything else.

584
00:27:21,750 --> 00:27:22,790
Thank you for your support.

585
00:27:23,590 --> 00:27:25,770
Now let's summarize the key phrases quickly.

586
00:27:26,450 --> 00:27:27,270
Do you have a moment?

587
00:27:27,670 --> 00:27:28,400
Polite opening.

588
00:27:29,240 --> 00:27:33,340
I need clarification.

589
00:27:33,360 --> 00:27:34,700
Unclear instruction.

590
00:27:35,460 --> 00:27:40,070
I've tried my best, but I'm stuck.

591
00:27:40,530 --> 00:27:42,350
Showing effort before asking for help.

592
00:27:43,070 --> 00:27:44,610
Can you point me in the right direction?

593
00:27:45,110 --> 00:27:47,130
Asking for guidance, not full answers.

594
00:27:47,910 --> 00:27:49,740
I want to make sure I'm on the right track.

595
00:27:50,180 --> 00:27:51,100
Checking progress.

596
00:27:51,680 --> 00:27:53,350
Thanks for joining today's episode.

597
00:27:54,030 --> 00:27:56,370
Practice these sentences in real situations.

598
00:27:56,650 --> 00:27:58,670
You will improve faster than you expect.

599
00:27:59,390 --> 00:28:02,130
And we'll see you in the next Business English Conversation.

600
00:28:02,800 --> 00:28:04,140
Take care.

601
00:28:04,160 --> 00:28:07,380
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to our English Learning Podcast.

602
00:28:07,720 --> 00:28:13,790
Today we have a very important topic for anyone who works in an office, company, store, school, or even online.

603
00:28:14,400 --> 00:28:14,870
That's right.

604
00:28:15,270 --> 00:28:19,870
Today we're going to talk about how to ask your boss for help in English.

605
00:28:20,020 --> 00:28:23,060
Many English learners feel nervous when talking to their boss.

606
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:25,380
Especially when they need help.

607
00:28:25,400 --> 00:28:29,330
Sometimes people worry about sounding weak, unprofessional, or impolite.

608
00:28:29,690 --> 00:28:33,250
But asking for help is actually a very important professional skill.

609
00:28:33,750 --> 00:28:34,370
Exactly.

610
00:28:34,690 --> 00:28:39,780
Good employees ask questions, communicate clearly, and solve problems together with their team.

611
00:28:40,200 --> 00:28:48,190
In today's podcast, we'll teach you useful English phrases, explain their meanings in simple English, and show practical conversations you can use at work.

612
00:28:48,170 --> 00:28:54,840
We'll also talk about polite language, confidence, professional communication, and common workplace situations.

613
00:28:54,980 --> 00:28:59,070
And don't worry, we'll use simple and natural English so you can understand easily.

614
00:28:59,510 --> 00:28:59,910
Perfect.

615
00:29:00,150 --> 00:29:00,650
Let's begin.

616
00:29:00,710 --> 00:29:02,250
Imagine this situation.

617
00:29:02,510 --> 00:29:06,270
You're working on a task, but suddenly you don't understand the instructions.

618
00:29:06,750 --> 00:29:09,280
Maybe your manager explained something too quickly.

619
00:29:09,700 --> 00:29:11,880
Or maybe this is your first time doing the task.

620
00:29:12,320 --> 00:29:14,800
Many people stay silent because they feel embarrassed.

621
00:29:15,160 --> 00:29:17,820
But staying silent can create bigger problems later.

622
00:29:18,340 --> 00:29:18,900
Exactly.

623
00:29:18,980 --> 00:29:23,810
So the first important phrase is, could you help me understand this task better?

624
00:29:23,790 --> 00:29:26,150
This is polite, professional, and honest.

625
00:29:26,600 --> 00:29:28,240
Let's hear an example conversation.

626
00:29:28,740 --> 00:29:29,860
Excuse me, Mr. Taylor.

627
00:29:29,900 --> 00:29:30,620
Do you have a minute?

628
00:29:30,990 --> 00:29:31,410
Of course.

629
00:29:31,570 --> 00:29:32,130
What do you need?

630
00:29:32,530 --> 00:29:34,570
Could you help me understand this task better?

631
00:29:34,600 --> 00:29:36,500
I want to make sure I do it correctly.

632
00:29:36,680 --> 00:29:37,320
Absolutely.

633
00:29:37,360 --> 00:29:38,980
Let me explain it step by step.

634
00:29:39,420 --> 00:29:39,630
See?

635
00:29:39,750 --> 00:29:41,330
That sounds professional, not weak.

636
00:29:41,850 --> 00:29:42,430
Exactly.

637
00:29:42,670 --> 00:29:46,070
Your boss will usually appreciate that you care about doing the work correctly.

638
00:29:46,450 --> 00:29:47,640
Here's another useful phrase.

639
00:29:48,140 --> 00:29:50,340
I'm not completely sure how to proceed.

640
00:29:50,760 --> 00:29:53,360
Proceed means continue or move forward.

641
00:29:53,840 --> 00:29:54,840
Example conversation.

642
00:29:55,310 --> 00:29:56,710
How's the client report going?

643
00:29:57,170 --> 00:30:02,550
I've finished the first section, but I'm not completely sure how to proceed with the financial analysis part.

644
00:30:02,770 --> 00:30:03,430
No problem.

645
00:30:03,670 --> 00:30:05,820
I'll show you an example from a previous report.

646
00:30:06,260 --> 00:30:08,420
That's a very natural business conversation.

647
00:30:08,880 --> 00:30:10,380
And notice something important.

648
00:30:10,660 --> 00:30:13,510
The employee first explained what they already completed.

649
00:30:13,930 --> 00:30:14,830
Good point.

650
00:30:14,850 --> 00:30:18,560
Bosses usually like when employees show effort before asking for help.

651
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:19,680
Exactly.

652
00:30:20,140 --> 00:30:22,690
Now let's talk about another common situation.

653
00:30:22,670 --> 00:30:24,970
Sometimes you are overwhelmed with work.

654
00:30:25,390 --> 00:30:29,460
Maybe you have too many deadlines, meetings and responsibilities at the same time.

655
00:30:29,880 --> 00:30:34,970
In that case, you can say, I could use some guidance on prioritizing my tasks.

656
00:30:34,990 --> 00:30:36,710
Guidance means help or advice.

657
00:30:37,190 --> 00:30:41,520
And prioritizing means deciding what is most important first.

658
00:30:41,540 --> 00:30:42,680
Example conversation.

659
00:30:42,920 --> 00:30:43,580
Hi, Sandra.

660
00:30:43,840 --> 00:30:45,660
I wanted to talk to you about my workload.

661
00:30:46,140 --> 00:30:46,360
Sure.

662
00:30:46,580 --> 00:30:47,160
What's going on?

663
00:30:47,540 --> 00:30:52,730
I'm currently handling three projects, and I could use some guidance on prioritizing my tasks.

664
00:30:53,310 --> 00:30:53,930
I understand.

665
00:30:54,350 --> 00:30:57,470
Let's review everything together and decide what's most urgent.

666
00:30:57,850 --> 00:30:59,640
That's a great professional conversation.

667
00:31:00,160 --> 00:31:01,700
Very respectful and clear.

668
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:08,630
Another useful phrase is, could you clarify what you mean by... Clarify means explain more clearly.

669
00:31:09,090 --> 00:31:10,090
Example conversation.

670
00:31:10,340 --> 00:31:13,020
Please make the presentation more client-focused.

671
00:31:13,500 --> 00:31:13,940
Of course.

672
00:31:14,360 --> 00:31:17,010
Could you clarify what you mean by client-focused?

673
00:31:17,510 --> 00:31:17,730
Sure.

674
00:31:17,750 --> 00:31:22,630
I want the presentation to focus more on customer benefits instead of technical details.

675
00:31:23,190 --> 00:31:25,800
Asking for clarification is smart, not embarrassing.

676
00:31:26,300 --> 00:31:26,880
Exactly.

677
00:31:27,200 --> 00:31:29,440
It prevents mistakes and confusion later.

678
00:31:29,560 --> 00:31:32,800
Now let's discuss a situation many employees experience.

679
00:31:33,240 --> 00:31:35,490
You receive a task you've never done before.

680
00:31:35,890 --> 00:31:38,590
This can feel stressful, especially in English.

681
00:31:38,610 --> 00:31:43,380
In that case, you can say, I haven't handled this before so I may need some support.

682
00:31:43,860 --> 00:31:44,840
Example conversation.

683
00:31:45,260 --> 00:31:47,500
Can you prepare the monthly inventory report?

684
00:31:47,980 --> 00:31:48,700
I'd be happy to.

685
00:31:48,720 --> 00:31:52,410
I haven't handled this before so I may need some support at the beginning.

686
00:31:52,580 --> 00:31:53,800
That's completely fine.

687
00:31:54,160 --> 00:31:56,120
Emily can help you learn the process today.

688
00:31:56,620 --> 00:31:57,940
See how professional that sounds?

689
00:31:58,480 --> 00:32:00,950
Yes, you sound responsible and honest.

690
00:32:01,410 --> 00:32:04,470
Another important phrase is, could we go over this together?

691
00:32:04,990 --> 00:32:07,920
Go over means review or check carefully.

692
00:32:08,380 --> 00:32:09,360
Example conversation.

693
00:32:09,760 --> 00:32:11,320
Did you finish the contract draft?

694
00:32:11,760 --> 00:32:15,570
Yes, but before sending it to the client, could we go over this together?

695
00:32:15,910 --> 00:32:17,030
I want to avoid mistakes.

696
00:32:17,550 --> 00:32:18,090
Absolutely.

697
00:32:18,310 --> 00:32:19,290
Let's review it now.

698
00:32:19,530 --> 00:32:21,430
Very professional teamwork language.

699
00:32:21,910 --> 00:32:22,450
Definitely.

700
00:32:22,910 --> 00:32:24,710
Now let's talk about confidence again.

701
00:32:25,170 --> 00:32:28,820
Many learners think fluent English means using difficult words.

702
00:32:29,260 --> 00:32:33,620
But professional English is actually about being clear, polite, and calm.

703
00:32:34,160 --> 00:32:34,790
Exactly.

704
00:32:35,150 --> 00:32:37,770
Simple English can still sound highly professional.

705
00:32:38,130 --> 00:32:40,030
For example, could you help me with this?

706
00:32:40,510 --> 00:32:43,180
Very simple, very natural, very professional.

707
00:32:43,580 --> 00:32:46,660
Sometimes simple communication is the strongest communication.

708
00:32:47,140 --> 00:32:48,060
Absolutely.

709
00:32:48,040 --> 00:32:51,330
Let's practice a longer, realistic office conversation now.

710
00:32:51,770 --> 00:32:52,350
Great idea.

711
00:32:52,770 --> 00:32:53,370
Hi, Daniel.

712
00:32:53,530 --> 00:32:54,350
Do you have a few minutes?

713
00:32:54,890 --> 00:32:55,130
Sure.

714
00:32:55,330 --> 00:32:55,590
Come in.

715
00:32:55,910 --> 00:32:56,510
What's going on?

716
00:32:57,010 --> 00:32:59,680
I wanted to ask for some help with the client presentation.

717
00:33:00,160 --> 00:33:03,560
I've prepared most of it, but I'm struggling with the financial section.

718
00:33:04,020 --> 00:33:04,360
Okay.

719
00:33:04,660 --> 00:33:05,700
What part is confusing?

720
00:33:06,180 --> 00:33:10,450
I'm not completely sure how to explain the quarterly growth numbers clearly.

721
00:33:10,990 --> 00:33:13,410
I want to make sure the client understands them easily.

722
00:33:13,830 --> 00:33:14,510
That makes sense.

723
00:33:14,910 --> 00:33:16,950
Have you looked at last quarter's presentation?

724
00:33:17,260 --> 00:33:20,720
Yes, I reviewed it this morning, but I'd still appreciate your guidance.

725
00:33:21,220 --> 00:33:21,800
No problem.

726
00:33:22,060 --> 00:33:23,540
Let's open the slides together.

727
00:33:24,010 --> 00:33:24,380
Thank you.

728
00:33:24,890 --> 00:33:27,270
I really want to improve my presentation skills, too.

729
00:33:27,750 --> 00:33:28,890
And you are improving.

730
00:33:29,150 --> 00:33:30,590
Don't be afraid to ask questions.

731
00:33:30,810 --> 00:33:31,880
That's how people learn.

732
00:33:32,360 --> 00:33:33,160
I appreciate that.

733
00:33:33,700 --> 00:33:36,380
Sometimes I worry about asking too many questions.

734
00:33:36,840 --> 00:33:41,250
Honestly, asking questions is much better than making unavoidable mistakes later.

735
00:33:41,670 --> 00:33:42,210
That's true.

736
00:33:42,650 --> 00:33:46,000
Let me show you a simpler way to explain these numbers to the client.

737
00:33:46,230 --> 00:33:47,390
That would be very helpful.

738
00:33:47,830 --> 00:33:52,120
See, you can focus on growth percentages instead of too many technical details.

739
00:33:52,820 --> 00:33:55,090
Oh, that actually makes much more sense now.

740
00:33:55,550 --> 00:33:55,890
Great.

741
00:33:56,130 --> 00:33:57,610
You're doing a good job overall.

742
00:33:58,030 --> 00:33:59,260
Thanks for your support, Daniel.

743
00:33:59,660 --> 00:34:00,100
Anytime.

744
00:34:00,580 --> 00:34:03,140
That was a very realistic workplace conversation.

745
00:34:03,580 --> 00:34:08,150
Yes, and notice how respectful, calm, and professional the employee sounded.

746
00:34:08,420 --> 00:34:12,330
No panic, no negative self-talk, just clear communication.

747
00:34:12,810 --> 00:34:13,410
Exactly.

748
00:34:13,790 --> 00:34:17,360
Before we finish, let's quickly review some important phrases from today.

749
00:34:17,800 --> 00:34:19,620
Could you help me understand this better?

750
00:34:20,040 --> 00:34:22,100
I'm not completely sure how to proceed.

751
00:34:22,620 --> 00:34:23,870
I could use some guidance.

752
00:34:24,330 --> 00:34:25,990
Could you clarify what you mean?

753
00:34:26,070 --> 00:34:27,430
Could we go over this together?

754
00:34:27,470 --> 00:34:29,090
I'd appreciate your advice.

755
00:34:29,550 --> 00:34:30,920
Do you have any suggestions?

756
00:34:31,420 --> 00:34:33,060
I'm feeling a little overwhelmed.

757
00:34:33,560 --> 00:34:35,360
I want to make sure I'm on the right track.

758
00:34:35,800 --> 00:34:37,260
Could you walk me through the process?

759
00:34:37,770 --> 00:34:41,250
These phrases will help you sound more natural and professional at work.

760
00:34:41,670 --> 00:34:43,870
Remember, asking for help is not a weakness.

761
00:34:44,350 --> 00:34:47,960
It shows responsibility, communication skills and professionalism.

762
00:34:48,340 --> 00:34:52,100
The more you practice these conversations, the more confident your English will become.

763
00:34:52,480 --> 00:34:54,630
Thank you so much for learning with us today.

764
00:34:54,610 --> 00:34:59,150
We hope this podcast helps you communicate more confidently with your boss and coworkers.

765
00:34:59,610 --> 00:35:03,040
Keep practicing your English every day, and don't be afraid to speak.

766
00:35:03,520 --> 00:35:06,560
See you next time with another practical business English podcast.

767
00:35:06,880 --> 00:35:09,220
Take care, everyone, and have a wonderful day at work.

768
00:35:09,830 --> 00:35:13,610
Good morning, and welcome to today's business English training session.

769
00:35:14,330 --> 00:35:21,660
In this episode, we will focus on a very important workplace skill, how to ask your boss for help in English.

770
00:35:22,060 --> 00:35:22,860
Hello, everyone.

771
00:35:23,340 --> 00:35:28,050
Many people feel nervous when talking to their boss, especially when they need help.

772
00:35:28,590 --> 00:35:36,560
But in real work situations, asking for help is normal, and doing it in the right way shows professionalism, not weakness.

773
00:35:37,160 --> 00:35:46,610
Today, we will guide you through simple English phrases, clear meanings, and real workplace conversations so you can confidently speak to your manager.

774
00:35:47,250 --> 00:35:47,890
Let's begin.

775
00:35:47,970 --> 00:35:51,700
First, let's understand the basic idea.

776
00:35:52,050 --> 00:36:01,620
Meaning, asking your boss for help means requesting guidance, support, or clarification when you are unsure about a task or decision.

777
00:36:02,300 --> 00:36:03,160
Important point.

778
00:36:03,760 --> 00:36:09,550
In business English, you should always be polite, clear, and respectful when speaking to your boss.

779
00:36:10,310 --> 00:36:13,180
Now let's move into real phrases you can use.

780
00:36:13,880 --> 00:36:16,060
First phrase, do you have a moment?

781
00:36:16,660 --> 00:36:20,610
This is a polite way to check if your boss is available to talk.

782
00:36:21,090 --> 00:36:22,030
Example usage.

783
00:36:22,910 --> 00:36:24,680
Excuse me, do you have a moment?

784
00:36:25,080 --> 00:36:27,380
I'm not sure how to finish this analysis.

785
00:36:28,060 --> 00:36:28,800
Yes, sure.

786
00:36:29,190 --> 00:36:30,950
Come in and let's look at it together.

787
00:36:30,970 --> 00:36:34,320
Next, could I ask for your help with something?

788
00:36:34,960 --> 00:36:38,440
This is a polite and professional way to request assistance.

789
00:36:39,200 --> 00:36:39,990
Example usage.

790
00:36:40,730 --> 00:36:43,570
Could I ask for your help with the client presentation?

791
00:36:44,060 --> 00:36:45,240
Conversation example.

792
00:36:46,480 --> 00:36:48,640
Hi, could I ask for your help with something?

793
00:36:49,200 --> 00:36:51,140
I'm stuck on the financial summary.

794
00:36:51,970 --> 00:36:52,370
Of course.

795
00:36:52,830 --> 00:36:54,510
Tell me what part is confusing.

796
00:36:55,230 --> 00:36:58,070
Another useful phrase, I need some guidance.

797
00:36:58,830 --> 00:37:04,220
Meaning, this means you need direction or advice, not a full solution.

798
00:37:04,980 --> 00:37:05,800
Example usage.

799
00:37:06,680 --> 00:37:09,640
I need some guidance on how to structure this report.

800
00:37:10,150 --> 00:37:14,410
Conversation example, I'm not sure if I'm doing this correctly.

801
00:37:14,810 --> 00:37:15,810
I need some guidance.

802
00:37:16,530 --> 00:37:17,410
That's okay.

803
00:37:17,430 --> 00:37:19,180
Let me show you the correct format.

804
00:37:19,860 --> 00:37:22,520
Next phrase, I'm not sure how to...

805
00:37:23,240 --> 00:37:26,040
This is a simple way to explain your confusion.

806
00:37:26,800 --> 00:37:31,350
Example usage, I'm not sure how to complete this task correctly.

807
00:37:31,990 --> 00:37:36,230
Conversation example, I'm not sure how to analyze this data.

808
00:37:36,510 --> 00:37:37,450
Can you help me?

809
00:37:37,740 --> 00:37:40,140
Yes, let's go through it step by step.

810
00:37:40,880 --> 00:37:41,970
Another important phrase.

811
00:37:42,430 --> 00:37:44,010
Could you clarify this for me?

812
00:37:44,030 --> 00:37:48,200
This means you want your boss to explain something more clearly.

813
00:37:48,240 --> 00:37:50,400
Example usage.

814
00:37:50,420 --> 00:37:53,090
Could you clarify the deadline for this project?

815
00:37:53,710 --> 00:37:54,850
Conversation example.

816
00:37:55,590 --> 00:37:59,660
Could you clarify what you mean by the final report requirements?

817
00:37:59,840 --> 00:38:00,200
Sure.

818
00:38:00,620 --> 00:38:04,390
I mean it should include sales, costs, and performance analysis.

819
00:38:05,130 --> 00:38:06,950
Now a very professional phrase.

820
00:38:07,490 --> 00:38:08,950
I'm having difficulty with.

821
00:38:09,670 --> 00:38:12,300
This is a polite way to say you are struggling.

822
00:38:13,080 --> 00:38:13,880
Example usage.

823
00:38:14,600 --> 00:38:17,200
I'm having difficulty with the new software system.

824
00:38:17,900 --> 00:38:18,980
Conversation example.

825
00:38:19,780 --> 00:38:22,890
I'm having difficulty understanding this client request.

826
00:38:23,670 --> 00:38:24,250
That's okay.

827
00:38:24,450 --> 00:38:25,550
Let's review it together.

828
00:38:25,590 --> 00:38:26,930
Next phrase.

829
00:38:27,550 --> 00:38:29,610
Could you point me in the right direction?

830
00:38:30,100 --> 00:38:33,950
This means you are asking for guidance, not full instructions.

831
00:38:34,730 --> 00:38:35,550
Example usage.

832
00:38:36,270 --> 00:38:39,110
Could you point me in the right direction for this project?

833
00:38:39,730 --> 00:38:40,890
Conversation example.

834
00:38:41,680 --> 00:38:43,120
I'm not sure where to start.

835
00:38:43,520 --> 00:38:45,080
Could you point me in the right direction?

836
00:38:45,920 --> 00:38:48,090
Start by reviewing last month's report.

837
00:38:48,750 --> 00:38:50,330
Now, another useful phrase.

838
00:38:51,110 --> 00:38:52,710
I would appreciate your advice.

839
00:38:53,430 --> 00:38:57,140
Meaning, this is a polite way to request suggestions.

840
00:38:57,570 --> 00:38:58,530
Example usage.

841
00:38:59,280 --> 00:39:03,040
I would appreciate your advice on how to handle this client issue.

842
00:39:03,720 --> 00:39:04,870
Conversation example.

843
00:39:05,710 --> 00:39:09,890
I would appreciate your advice before I respond to the customer email.

844
00:39:10,620 --> 00:39:12,200
Sure, let me read it first.

845
00:39:12,940 --> 00:39:15,630
Now let's look at a real workplace situation.

846
00:39:16,310 --> 00:39:19,270
Imagine you are behind on a project deadline.

847
00:39:19,810 --> 00:39:20,700
You can say...

848
00:39:21,050 --> 00:39:25,270
I wanted to let you know I'm slightly behind on this task.

849
00:39:25,290 --> 00:39:27,750
I'm having some difficulty with the data analysis.

850
00:39:28,010 --> 00:39:29,110
Could you help me review it?

851
00:39:29,800 --> 00:39:30,920
Conversation example.

852
00:39:31,720 --> 00:39:34,180
I'm not making as much progress as expected.

853
00:39:34,560 --> 00:39:35,960
Could you help me with this part?

854
00:39:36,660 --> 00:39:37,080
Of course.

855
00:39:37,400 --> 00:39:38,470
Let's fix it together.

856
00:39:39,120 --> 00:39:42,390
Another situation, when you don't understand instructions.

857
00:39:43,090 --> 00:39:46,750
You can say, I want to make sure I do this correctly.

858
00:39:47,090 --> 00:39:48,740
Could you explain it one more time?

859
00:39:49,400 --> 00:39:50,920
Conversation example.

860
00:39:51,220 --> 00:39:54,100
I just want to confirm I understand this properly.

861
00:39:54,400 --> 00:39:56,060
Could you explain the process again?

862
00:39:56,100 --> 00:39:57,060
Sure.

863
00:39:57,080 --> 00:39:58,830
I'll walk you through it slowly.

864
00:39:58,850 --> 00:40:01,290
Now, let's talk about tone.

865
00:40:01,710 --> 00:40:02,870
This is very important.

866
00:40:03,550 --> 00:40:09,640
When speaking to your boss, always use polite words like could, would, and please.

867
00:40:09,680 --> 00:40:12,960
Instead of saying, I don't understand this.

868
00:40:13,700 --> 00:40:16,300
Say, I'm not sure I understand this.

869
00:40:16,660 --> 00:40:17,290
Could you help me?

870
00:40:17,350 --> 00:40:20,450
This sounds more professional and respectful.

871
00:40:20,870 --> 00:40:23,030
Another important tip is timing.

872
00:40:23,750 --> 00:40:28,600
Meaning, don't interrupt your boss when they are very busy unless it is urgent.

873
00:40:29,300 --> 00:40:31,730
You can say, sorry to interrupt.

874
00:40:32,010 --> 00:40:32,910
Do you have a moment?

875
00:40:33,550 --> 00:40:34,110
That's okay.

876
00:40:34,350 --> 00:40:34,730
Go ahead.

877
00:40:35,370 --> 00:40:38,660
Now let's look at asking for help in email form.

878
00:40:38,940 --> 00:40:39,940
Example email.

879
00:40:40,620 --> 00:40:41,530
I hope you are well.

880
00:40:42,070 --> 00:40:47,470
I am currently working on the client report and I'm having some difficulty with the financial section.

881
00:40:48,030 --> 00:40:50,120
Could you please guide me when you have time?

882
00:40:50,660 --> 00:40:53,080
I would really appreciate your help.

883
00:40:53,100 --> 00:40:53,460
Meaning.

884
00:40:54,040 --> 00:40:58,110
This is a polite and structured way to ask for help without pressure.

885
00:40:58,770 --> 00:41:00,050
Another email example.

886
00:41:00,750 --> 00:41:05,220
If possible, could you please review my presentation and share your feedback?

887
00:41:05,800 --> 00:41:08,440
I want to make sure it meets expectations.

888
00:41:08,830 --> 00:41:11,170
This shows respect and professionalism.

889
00:41:11,950 --> 00:41:14,680
Now, let's talk about what not to say.

890
00:41:15,420 --> 00:41:19,960
Avoid direct or rude phrases like, I don't know what to do.

891
00:41:19,980 --> 00:41:21,580
I can't do this.

892
00:41:21,640 --> 00:41:23,310
This is too hard.

893
00:41:24,070 --> 00:41:26,630
Instead, always reframe it politely.

894
00:41:27,510 --> 00:41:28,490
I need some guidance.

895
00:41:29,150 --> 00:41:30,520
I would appreciate your help.

896
00:41:31,280 --> 00:41:33,920
I'm having some difficulty with this part.

897
00:41:33,940 --> 00:41:37,360
Let's do one full workplace conversation now.

898
00:41:37,730 --> 00:41:37,990
Hi.

899
00:41:38,290 --> 00:41:39,170
Do you have a moment?

900
00:41:39,610 --> 00:41:41,580
I need some help with the project report.

901
00:41:42,240 --> 00:41:42,500
Sure.

902
00:41:42,560 --> 00:41:43,460
What's the issue?

903
00:41:44,220 --> 00:41:47,220
I'm not sure how to present the financial data clearly.

904
00:41:47,520 --> 00:41:48,260
Could you guide me?

905
00:41:48,920 --> 00:41:49,180
Yes.

906
00:41:49,600 --> 00:41:52,630
You should use charts and summarize the key numbers.

907
00:41:53,430 --> 00:41:54,310
That really helps.

908
00:41:54,550 --> 00:41:54,910
Thank you.

909
00:41:55,550 --> 00:41:56,090
No problem.

910
00:41:56,510 --> 00:41:58,010
Always feel free to ask.

911
00:41:58,750 --> 00:42:00,090
Another conversation example.

912
00:42:00,760 --> 00:42:01,260
Excuse me.

913
00:42:01,610 --> 00:42:02,920
Could I ask for your advice?

914
00:42:03,700 --> 00:42:04,060
Of course.

915
00:42:04,420 --> 00:42:05,480
What do you need help with?

916
00:42:05,850 --> 00:42:09,190
I'm having difficulty prioritizing my tasks this week.

917
00:42:09,970 --> 00:42:12,720
Let's go through your list and organize it together.

918
00:42:13,360 --> 00:42:13,740
Thank you.

919
00:42:14,080 --> 00:42:15,680
I really appreciate your support.

920
00:42:16,380 --> 00:42:17,960
To summarize today's lesson.

921
00:42:18,660 --> 00:42:23,530
Asking your boss for help is normal, professional, and expected in the workplace.

922
00:42:24,230 --> 00:42:28,610
The key is to be polite, clear, and respectful in your communication.

923
00:42:29,410 --> 00:42:35,600
Use phrases like, could you help me, I need guidance, and could you clarify this for me?

924
00:42:36,050 --> 00:42:39,630
And remember, good communication builds trust with your manager.

925
00:42:40,250 --> 00:42:42,030
Thank you for joining today's session.

926
00:42:42,380 --> 00:42:45,030
Keep practicing these phrases in your daily work life.

927
00:42:45,750 --> 00:42:47,090
See you in the next episode.

928
00:42:47,380 --> 00:42:53,420
Welcome, everyone, to today's English Learning Podcast for professionals and workplace communication.

929
00:42:53,920 --> 00:42:55,470
Thank you for joining us today.

930
00:42:55,810 --> 00:42:59,970
We're excited to help you improve your English in a practical and natural way.

931
00:43:00,450 --> 00:43:00,970
I'm Ryan.

932
00:43:01,270 --> 00:43:01,990
And I'm Chloe.

933
00:43:02,330 --> 00:43:11,060
Today's topic is very important for anyone who works in an office, company, shop, restaurant, or any professional environment.

934
00:43:11,440 --> 00:43:11,940
That's right.

935
00:43:12,100 --> 00:43:16,390
Today we're talking about how to ask your boss for help in English.

936
00:43:16,370 --> 00:43:22,750
Many English learners feel nervous when speaking to their manager or supervisor, especially when they need help.

937
00:43:23,290 --> 00:43:25,500
Some people worry about sounding weak,

938
00:43:25,630 --> 00:43:29,320
Others worry about using the wrong words or sounding unprofessional.

939
00:43:29,640 --> 00:43:33,910
But asking for help is actually a normal and important part of work.

940
00:43:34,330 --> 00:43:38,030
Even experienced employees ask questions and request support.

941
00:43:38,340 --> 00:43:42,840
The key is knowing how to ask politely, clearly, and professionally.

942
00:43:43,240 --> 00:43:49,610
And today, we'll teach you exactly how to do that using simple English and real workplace conversations.

943
00:43:49,730 --> 00:43:55,920
We'll explain useful phrases, their meanings, and practical situations you can use at work immediately.

944
00:43:56,320 --> 00:43:59,180
So let's begin with the first and most important thing.

945
00:43:59,620 --> 00:44:01,580
Don't be afraid to ask for help.

946
00:44:02,070 --> 00:44:06,210
Good managers usually appreciate employees who communicate honestly.

947
00:44:06,590 --> 00:44:10,390
It's much better to ask questions than to stay silent and make mistakes.

948
00:44:10,910 --> 00:44:11,520
Exactly.

949
00:44:11,840 --> 00:44:14,420
Communication builds trust at work.

950
00:44:14,440 --> 00:44:18,180
So let's start with one of the simplest and most useful phrases.

951
00:44:18,160 --> 00:44:19,450
Could you help me with this?

952
00:44:19,930 --> 00:44:22,570
Meaning, you politely ask for assistance.

953
00:44:23,050 --> 00:44:25,680
This phrase is simple, direct, and professional.

954
00:44:26,080 --> 00:44:27,280
Example conversation.

955
00:44:27,740 --> 00:44:28,680
Hi, Mr. Adams.

956
00:44:28,900 --> 00:44:30,300
Could you help me with this report?

957
00:44:30,660 --> 00:44:31,060
Of course.

958
00:44:31,610 --> 00:44:32,570
What do you need help with?

959
00:44:33,010 --> 00:44:36,050
I'm not sure how to organize the sales data correctly.

960
00:44:36,490 --> 00:44:37,190
No problem.

961
00:44:37,430 --> 00:44:39,100
Let me show you the best format.

962
00:44:39,580 --> 00:44:40,040
Thank you.

963
00:44:40,240 --> 00:44:41,340
I really appreciate it.

964
00:44:41,780 --> 00:44:45,390
Notice how polite and natural that conversation sounds.

965
00:44:45,370 --> 00:44:48,050
Yes, you don't need difficult vocabulary.

966
00:44:48,550 --> 00:44:52,320
Simple and clear English works very well in business situations.

967
00:44:52,800 --> 00:44:59,560
Another excellent phrase is, Meaning, you politely ask if your boss has time to talk.

968
00:45:00,020 --> 00:45:02,460
This is a respectful way to start a conversation.

969
00:45:02,950 --> 00:45:04,170
Example Conversation

970
00:45:04,430 --> 00:45:05,140
Hi, Sarah.

971
00:45:05,280 --> 00:45:06,160
Do you have a moment?

972
00:45:06,640 --> 00:45:06,900
Sure.

973
00:45:07,160 --> 00:45:07,520
What's up?

974
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:10,480
I need some guidance on the new client project.

975
00:45:10,900 --> 00:45:11,300
Of course.

976
00:45:11,800 --> 00:45:12,720
Let's talk about it.

977
00:45:13,240 --> 00:45:13,860
Thanks.

978
00:45:13,880 --> 00:45:15,510
I wasn't sure how to move forward.

979
00:45:15,950 --> 00:45:17,690
Very professional and polite.

980
00:45:18,090 --> 00:45:19,530
Here's another useful phrase.

981
00:45:20,030 --> 00:45:21,730
I'm having trouble with...

982
00:45:21,750 --> 00:45:24,840
Meaning, you explain that something is difficult for you.

983
00:45:25,260 --> 00:45:26,420
Example conversation.

984
00:45:27,260 --> 00:45:32,600
Honestly, I'm having trouble with the financial section.

985
00:45:32,580 --> 00:45:33,160
I see.

986
00:45:33,200 --> 00:45:34,870
Which part is confusing?

987
00:45:35,330 --> 00:45:38,590
I'm not sure how to explain the quarterly numbers clearly.

988
00:45:39,070 --> 00:45:40,300
Let's review it together.

989
00:45:40,800 --> 00:45:41,860
Thank you so much.

990
00:45:42,400 --> 00:45:46,230
This phrase is very natural because it sounds honest and professional.

991
00:45:46,670 --> 00:45:49,810
Another common phrase is, could you explain this to me?

992
00:45:49,850 --> 00:45:53,800
Meaning, you need clarification or more information.

993
00:45:53,930 --> 00:45:55,190
Example conversation.

994
00:45:55,550 --> 00:45:57,750
Did you understand the new company policy?

995
00:45:58,230 --> 00:45:59,110
Not completely.

996
00:45:59,530 --> 00:46:01,060
Could you explain this to me again?

997
00:46:01,120 --> 00:46:01,860
Sure.

998
00:46:02,320 --> 00:46:06,740
The policy mainly affects vacation requests and remote work schedules.

999
00:46:07,420 --> 00:46:09,080
Oh, now I understand better.

1000
00:46:09,520 --> 00:46:09,800
Great.

1001
00:46:10,300 --> 00:46:13,170
Asking for clarification is very important at work.

1002
00:46:13,590 --> 00:46:14,170
Definitely.

1003
00:46:14,190 --> 00:46:16,990
Never pretend to understand when you don't.

1004
00:46:17,470 --> 00:46:20,490
Another useful phrase is, I could use some advice.

1005
00:46:20,990 --> 00:46:23,940
Meaning, you want suggestions or guidance.

1006
00:46:23,920 --> 00:46:25,200
Example conversation.

1007
00:46:25,740 --> 00:46:29,660
Hi David, I could use some advice about dealing with this customer complaint.

1008
00:46:30,120 --> 00:46:30,540
Of course.

1009
00:46:31,070 --> 00:46:32,010
Tell me what happened.

1010
00:46:32,450 --> 00:46:35,530
The customer is upset about a delayed shipment.

1011
00:46:35,550 --> 00:46:40,270
In that case, focus on apologizing first and offering solutions calmly.

1012
00:46:40,800 --> 00:46:41,620
That makes sense.

1013
00:46:41,900 --> 00:46:42,820
Thanks for the advice.

1014
00:46:43,360 --> 00:46:49,380
Notice that I could use some advice sounds softer and more natural than simply saying help me.

1015
00:46:49,470 --> 00:46:52,750
Yes, it sounds more professional in office conversations.

1016
00:46:53,230 --> 00:46:58,220
Another important workplace phrase is, I'm not sure how to handle this situation.

1017
00:46:58,660 --> 00:47:01,480
Meaning, you don't know the best way to solve a problem.

1018
00:47:01,900 --> 00:47:03,000
Example conversation.

1019
00:47:03,440 --> 00:47:06,970
Karen, I'm not sure how to handle this situation with the supplier.

1020
00:47:07,330 --> 00:47:08,590
What seems to be the issue?

1021
00:47:09,090 --> 00:47:11,950
They missed another delivery deadline this week.

1022
00:47:11,970 --> 00:47:12,610
I understand.

1023
00:47:13,170 --> 00:47:16,040
Let's discuss the best way to respond professionally.

1024
00:47:16,500 --> 00:47:17,600
I'd appreciate that.

1025
00:47:17,800 --> 00:47:20,990
This type of language shows maturity and professionalism.

1026
00:47:21,460 --> 00:47:22,040
Exactly.

1027
00:47:22,420 --> 00:47:23,910
Here's another helpful phrase.

1028
00:47:24,410 --> 00:47:26,020
Can you walk me through it?

1029
00:47:26,050 --> 00:47:29,430
Meaning, please explain the process step by step.

1030
00:47:29,890 --> 00:47:30,960
Example conversation.

1031
00:47:31,440 --> 00:47:33,440
I've never used this software before.

1032
00:47:33,680 --> 00:47:34,760
Can you walk me through it?

1033
00:47:35,200 --> 00:47:35,760
Absolutely.

1034
00:47:36,200 --> 00:47:39,390
First, log into the system using your employee account.

1035
00:47:39,890 --> 00:47:40,250
Okay.

1036
00:47:40,670 --> 00:47:45,180
Then, upload the files here and submit the report through this tab.

1037
00:47:45,640 --> 00:47:48,160
That actually looks easier than I expected.

1038
00:47:48,260 --> 00:47:52,260
Many employees use this phrase when learning new systems or procedures.

1039
00:47:52,740 --> 00:47:55,410
It's one of the most practical business English phrases.

1040
00:47:55,950 --> 00:48:00,410
Another excellent phrase is, I want to make sure I understand correctly.

1041
00:48:00,820 --> 00:48:04,480
Meaning, you are checking information carefully to avoid mistakes.

1042
00:48:05,020 --> 00:48:06,120
Example conversation.

1043
00:48:06,600 --> 00:48:08,990
I want to make sure I understand correctly.

1044
00:48:09,410 --> 00:48:11,890
You need the report by Thursday morning, right?

1045
00:48:12,230 --> 00:48:12,590
Correct.

1046
00:48:13,110 --> 00:48:15,520
Thursday before 10 a.m. Great.

1047
00:48:15,840 --> 00:48:16,900
I'll prioritize it.

1048
00:48:17,260 --> 00:48:17,640
Perfect.

1049
00:48:17,970 --> 00:48:21,100
This phrase is excellent because it shows responsibility.

1050
00:48:21,500 --> 00:48:25,210
Managers usually appreciate employees who double-check details.

1051
00:48:25,790 --> 00:48:26,450
Absolutely.

1052
00:48:26,930 --> 00:48:29,710
Let's continue with another common office phrase.

1053
00:48:30,200 --> 00:48:31,600
Could we go over this together?

1054
00:48:31,640 --> 00:48:34,460
Meaning, can we review something together?

1055
00:48:34,900 --> 00:48:36,040
Example conversation.

1056
00:48:36,660 --> 00:48:39,510
Have you finished preparing the presentation slides?

1057
00:48:39,690 --> 00:48:43,100
Almost, but could we go over them together before the meeting?

1058
00:48:43,560 --> 00:48:44,040
Of course.

1059
00:48:44,500 --> 00:48:45,980
Let's review them this afternoon.

1060
00:48:46,040 --> 00:48:46,840
Thank you.

1061
00:48:47,340 --> 00:48:49,490
I want to make sure everything looks professional.

1062
00:48:49,910 --> 00:48:50,730
Great attitude.

1063
00:48:51,210 --> 00:48:58,160
Another useful phrase is, I'd appreciate your input, meaning you want your boss's opinion or suggestions.

1064
00:48:58,700 --> 00:49:00,400
Example conversation.

1065
00:49:00,380 --> 00:49:03,350
I've prepared two marketing ideas for the campaign.

1066
00:49:03,830 --> 00:49:05,150
I'd appreciate your input.

1067
00:49:05,530 --> 00:49:05,790
Sure.

1068
00:49:06,310 --> 00:49:07,550
Let's take a look.

1069
00:49:07,570 --> 00:49:09,780
I'm not sure which direction is stronger.

1070
00:49:10,320 --> 00:49:13,400
I think the second option fits our target audience better.

1071
00:49:13,900 --> 00:49:15,190
That's helpful feedback.

1072
00:49:15,590 --> 00:49:17,810
Here's another important phrase for the workplace.

1073
00:49:18,310 --> 00:49:20,380
I may need some support with this project.

1074
00:49:20,840 --> 00:49:24,200
Meaning, the task is difficult and you may need assistance.

1075
00:49:24,720 --> 00:49:26,300
Example conversation.

1076
00:49:26,280 --> 00:49:28,210
How's the new project coming along?

1077
00:49:28,730 --> 00:49:32,570
It's going well, but I may need some support with the research section.

1078
00:49:33,030 --> 00:49:33,660
No problem.

1079
00:49:33,990 --> 00:49:36,200
We can assign another team member to help you.

1080
00:49:36,580 --> 00:49:37,600
That would really help.

1081
00:49:38,020 --> 00:49:38,400
Thank you.

1082
00:49:38,940 --> 00:49:42,750
Asking for support early is much better than waiting until the last minute.

1083
00:49:43,110 --> 00:49:43,630
Exactly.

1084
00:49:44,030 --> 00:49:46,830
Good communication prevents bigger problems later.

1085
00:49:47,310 --> 00:49:50,660
Another very natural phrase is, I'm still learning.

1086
00:49:51,140 --> 00:49:54,720
Meaning, you are honest about being new or inexperienced.

1087
00:49:54,850 --> 00:49:56,080
Example conversation.

1088
00:49:56,600 --> 00:49:58,020
Sorry for all the questions.

1089
00:49:58,280 --> 00:49:59,640
I'm still learning the system.

1090
00:50:00,040 --> 00:50:01,490
That's completely okay.

1091
00:50:01,950 --> 00:50:03,630
Everyone learns at a different pace.

1092
00:50:04,150 --> 00:50:05,590
I appreciate your patience.

1093
00:50:06,130 --> 00:50:07,030
No problem at all.

1094
00:50:07,470 --> 00:50:09,860
This phrase sounds humble and professional.

1095
00:50:10,160 --> 00:50:10,720
Definitely.

1096
00:50:11,180 --> 00:50:14,830
Another useful phrase is, can you point me in the right direction?

1097
00:50:15,230 --> 00:50:18,450
Meaning, can you guide me or tell me how to begin?

1098
00:50:18,510 --> 00:50:20,570
Example conversation.

1099
00:50:20,550 --> 00:50:23,480
I'm not sure where to start with this client request.

1100
00:50:23,920 --> 00:50:25,580
Can you point me in the right direction?

1101
00:50:26,060 --> 00:50:26,320
Sure.

1102
00:50:26,800 --> 00:50:30,450
Start by reviewing their previous orders and communication history.

1103
00:50:30,870 --> 00:50:31,230
Got it.

1104
00:50:31,410 --> 00:50:32,310
That helps a lot.

1105
00:50:32,690 --> 00:50:33,050
Excellent.

1106
00:50:33,530 --> 00:50:35,510
Let's talk about something important now.

1107
00:50:35,930 --> 00:50:36,390
What's that?

1108
00:50:36,850 --> 00:50:37,650
Tone of voice.

1109
00:50:38,410 --> 00:50:38,860
Ah, yes.

1110
00:50:39,380 --> 00:50:42,900
Tone is extremely important when asking your boss for help.

1111
00:50:43,170 --> 00:50:46,270
Even correct English can sound rude if your tone is wrong.

1112
00:50:46,670 --> 00:50:50,540
For example, saying, I don't understand this, can sound negative.

1113
00:50:50,960 --> 00:50:55,370
But saying, could you explain this part to me, sounds polite and professional.

1114
00:50:55,870 --> 00:50:56,530
Exactly.

1115
00:50:56,910 --> 00:50:59,220
Small changes make a big difference.

1116
00:50:59,200 --> 00:51:01,320
Another important tip is timing.

1117
00:51:01,880 --> 00:51:02,120
Right.

1118
00:51:02,480 --> 00:51:06,530
Try not to interrupt your boss during stressful moments or important meetings.

1119
00:51:07,050 --> 00:51:13,320
Instead, use polite opening phrases like when you have a moment or is now a good time.

1120
00:51:13,780 --> 00:51:15,390
Example conversation.

1121
00:51:15,360 --> 00:51:16,190
Hi, Jennifer.

1122
00:51:16,410 --> 00:51:18,590
Is now a good time to ask a quick question?

1123
00:51:19,070 --> 00:51:19,310
Sure.

1124
00:51:19,720 --> 00:51:20,300
What do you need?

1125
00:51:20,780 --> 00:51:23,320
I need help understanding the updated schedule.

1126
00:51:23,780 --> 00:51:24,200
Of course.

1127
00:51:24,630 --> 00:51:25,750
Let's review it together.

1128
00:51:26,210 --> 00:51:26,670
Thank you.

1129
00:51:27,150 --> 00:51:30,640
Another workplace skill is explaining your problem clearly.

1130
00:51:30,890 --> 00:51:33,330
Yes, don't just say, I need help.

1131
00:51:33,810 --> 00:51:36,780
Explain the situation briefly and specifically.

1132
00:51:37,220 --> 00:51:41,080
For example, I'm having trouble organizing the customer database.

1133
00:51:41,620 --> 00:51:44,870
Or, I'm not sure how to respond to this client email.

1134
00:51:45,270 --> 00:51:48,240
Clear communication helps your boss help you faster.

1135
00:51:48,740 --> 00:51:49,280
Exactly.

1136
00:51:49,760 --> 00:51:52,860
Let's look at a longer practical workplace conversation.

1137
00:51:53,300 --> 00:51:53,980
Great idea.

1138
00:51:54,440 --> 00:51:55,660
Example conversation.

1139
00:51:55,950 --> 00:51:56,630
Hi, Michael.

1140
00:51:56,810 --> 00:51:57,850
Do you have a few minutes?

1141
00:51:58,410 --> 00:51:58,670
Sure.

1142
00:51:58,910 --> 00:51:59,170
Come in.

1143
00:51:59,640 --> 00:52:00,240
What's going on?

1144
00:52:00,700 --> 00:52:05,410
I'm working on the monthly report, but I'm having trouble understanding the new sales figures.

1145
00:52:05,950 --> 00:52:06,370
Okay.

1146
00:52:06,690 --> 00:52:07,890
Which part is confusing?

1147
00:52:08,310 --> 00:52:12,440
I'm not sure how to compare this quarter's numbers with last quarter's data.

1148
00:52:12,900 --> 00:52:13,600
I understand.

1149
00:52:13,660 --> 00:52:17,160
Let me show you a simpler way to organize the information.

1150
00:52:17,140 --> 00:52:18,970
That would be really helpful.

1151
00:52:19,370 --> 00:52:26,780
First, separate the regional sales into categories, then compare growth percentages instead of total revenue only.

1152
00:52:27,620 --> 00:52:29,650
Oh, that makes much more sense now.

1153
00:52:30,030 --> 00:52:30,230
Good.

1154
00:52:30,550 --> 00:52:31,710
You're actually doing well.

1155
00:52:32,150 --> 00:52:34,700
The new reporting system can be confusing at first.

1156
00:52:35,220 --> 00:52:36,420
I appreciate your help.

1157
00:52:36,740 --> 00:52:38,880
I didn't want to make mistakes in the report.

1158
00:52:39,280 --> 00:52:41,820
You did the right thing by asking questions early.

1159
00:52:42,320 --> 00:52:42,660
Thanks.

1160
00:52:42,940 --> 00:52:44,640
I feel much more confident now.

1161
00:52:45,110 --> 00:52:45,720
Excellent work.

1162
00:52:46,210 --> 00:52:47,670
Let me know if you need anything else.

1163
00:52:48,170 --> 00:52:48,510
I will.

1164
00:52:48,810 --> 00:52:49,350
Thanks again.

1165
00:52:49,410 --> 00:52:53,650
That conversation sounds natural, professional, and respectful.

1166
00:52:54,130 --> 00:52:57,460
Exactly the kind of communication many workplaces expect.

1167
00:52:57,860 --> 00:53:02,100
Before we finish, let's quickly review some of today's useful phrases.

1168
00:53:02,270 --> 00:53:03,490
Could you help me with this?

1169
00:53:03,970 --> 00:53:04,870
Do you have a moment?

1170
00:53:05,350 --> 00:53:08,720
I'm having trouble with... Could you explain this part to me?

1171
00:53:09,180 --> 00:53:10,320
I could use some advice.

1172
00:53:10,820 --> 00:53:12,100
Can you walk me through it?

1173
00:53:12,620 --> 00:53:15,010
I want to make sure I understand correctly.

1174
00:53:15,450 --> 00:53:16,730
I'd appreciate your input.

1175
00:53:17,250 --> 00:53:19,370
I may need some support with this project.

1176
00:53:19,790 --> 00:53:21,610
Can you point me in the right direction?

1177
00:53:22,050 --> 00:53:25,180
These are practical phrases you can use immediately at work.

1178
00:53:25,620 --> 00:53:28,540
And remember, asking for help is not weakness.

1179
00:53:29,040 --> 00:53:30,490
It shows responsibility...

1180
00:53:30,640 --> 00:53:33,040
communication skills, and professionalism.

1181
00:53:33,400 --> 00:53:37,690
Good employees ask questions because they care about doing quality work.

1182
00:53:38,110 --> 00:53:58,990
The more you practice these conversations, the more confident you'll become.
